-- title:
--   Transposing an n×m integer array stored on a stack
-- statement:
--   Let $L$ be a list of $n\cdot m$ integers on stack `XD` ($m \ge 1$, $nm \le 2^{kb}$), and registers $a, b$ (distinct from the scratch stacks 1,2,3) and `KBR` hold $n$, $m$, $kb$. If all entries and $n,m,kb$ have bit length $\le W$, the program `trWith a b` replaces $L$ by its transpose `transposeL n m L` (row-major $n\times m$ to row-major $m\times n$) within cost $10^6(kb+1)(nm+1)\cdot 2(W+kb+5) + 20000(W+2)$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The transpose is implemented by tagging every entry with its target index, a radix sort on the tags, and stripping the tags. `transposeL` is from `Def_IntMul_HvdH_MachineRef`. The machine layer: `IState HS` is a state with $64$ stacks of items (`Item` = integer or mark), an input position and an output; `RM` programs (straight-line register code `RS`, loops `rep`, conditionals, calls of the multiplication procedure through a `MulSpec` $M$ giving its domain and cost) have a functional semantics `p.run s`, a definedness predicate `p.ok M s` and a cost `p.cost M s`; `Spec M p s s' c` abbreviates `p.ok M s ∧ p.run s = s' ∧ p.cost M s ≤ c`. `ints L` / `gints U` push an integer list / a list of pairs as items (head = top); `zsz z` is the bit length of $|z|$. `MulBnd M m Φ` says the multiplication procedure accepts all pairs of integers of bit length $\le m$ at cost $\le Φ$. Register names (`GU`, `CR`, `KB`, `XN`, …) are fixed stack indices.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.FW.trWith_spec` of the Lean development NLogN/HFW4.lean (lines 38–67), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

